nodecode-git-review is a rewrite of mister-ideas's mister-ideas/git-review:

  https://github.com/mister-ideas/git-review
  commit a1944377c3f8d5699668b67379be9bf63ca0cee7
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 mister-ideas (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the read side of the review pane as two model-facing tools and a
command. The same five scopes the pane computes (unstaged, staged, uncommitted,
branch, commits) are computed here with the same git plumbing -- rev-parse,
status, diff, merge-base, log, show, ls-files -- with explicit repository
context, literal pathspecs, bounded context lines and one timeout per command.
The unified patch the pane paints is answered as text, with a per-file churn
summary read out of the patch itself, and new untracked files rendered as their
own new-file hunks. The write path is the pane's commit box: stage (all or
named paths), commit with the repository's hooks off, push (publishing a branch
that has no upstream), and create a pull or merge request with gh or glab. The
pane's read_only setting, its base ref, its ignore-whitespace flag and its
display ceilings are the cell's section. The repository's LICENSE is unchanged
beside this file.

Left out: everything that is drawing rather than computing -- the split layout,
syntax colours, folding and expanding unchanged runs, the file tree and its
search, drag-to-cite into the composer, the status bar, the keyboard shortcut
and the persisted display state. Also left out: the "Last agent turn" scope and
its snapshot machinery (an index and object database of the plugin's own,
written at every message), the AI-drafted commit message that rides the host's
one-shot template, the staged/unstaged per-file discard, the dashboard and its
HTTP routes, and the pull-request lookup that runs gh or glab on every refresh
(here a request is created on ask, and an existing one is not looked up).
